t: remove items you don\u2019t use. It makes moving easier and reveals what storage you actually need.\n\nPlan the layout\n3. Prioritize the bed as the focal point: place the headboard against a solid wall (not under a window if you want less light/noise). Aim for symmetrical bedside clearance (at least 24\u201330 in walking space if possible).\n4. Create zones: sleeping (bed), dressing (dresser + mirror), work/entertainment (desk/TV). Keep the work/TV zone separate from the bed to help reduce bedtime screen use.\n5. Align furniture with traffic flow: keep a clear path from door to bed and to closet; avoid blocking radiators/vents and outlets.\n\nWi\u2011Fi and electronics\n6. Position electronics with connectivity in mind: avoid putting the laptop/TV behind thick walls or inside large metal\u2011framed furniture that could block signal. If the living\u2011room extender helps, place bedroom devices on the same band as that extender or test placement before finalizing.\n7. Test signal after moving: temporarily place your laptop/phone in the new spots and check speeds/connection before committing.\n\nSmart home and lighting\n8. Place the Echo Show where voice pickup is reliable (not tucked behind pillows or turned away). Put smart bulbs/lamps with warm color temps in reading areas and on dim schedules to support sleep hygiene.\n9. Create a reading nook: a comfortable chair or bench, adjustable warm lamp, and a small side table to encourage reading instead of watching TV in bed.\n\nTV/computer & cable management\n10. Reduce screens in the sleeping area: if you want to cut screen time, consider mounting the TV on a wall opposite or to the side of the bed (or removing it from the bedroom altogether).\n11. Manage cables as you move: use zip/Velcro ties to bundle, adhesive cable clips to route cords, a cable raceway or cord cover for visible runs, and a surge-protected power strip tucked in a bin or behind furniture. Consider a slim power/USB hub at the bedside.\n12. If wall\u2011mounting the TV, plan for cable concealment (in\u2011wall kit or surface raceway) and ensure safe access to outlets.\n\nAesthetics & mid\u2011century styling\n13. Keep it low and airy: use lower-profile furniture (dresser with tapered legs) and leave negative space to get that mid\u2011century look.\n14. Balance and proportion: place the dresser so it\u2019s visible but not blocking windows or doors. A mirror above the dresser is great for dressing and makes the room feel larger; place so it doesn\u2019t reflect bright lights into the bed at night.\n15. Hardware and finishes: if your dresser is walnut with brass knobs and satin finish, let it be a visual anchor \u2014 place it where it can be admired without crowding.\n\nErgonomics & storage\n16. Nightstand essentials: place one within arm\u2019s reach of the bed for a lamp, book, glass of water, and phone charger. Keep high\u2011use items accessible.\n17. Ensure drawer clearance: drawers must be able to open fully without hitting doors or other furniture.\n18. Use vertical storage: floating shelves, a slim bookshelf, or wall hooks free floor space.\n\nFinishing touches & test\n19. Try temporary layouts first: move heavier pieces last \u2014 use furniture sliders to protect floors. Live with a layout for a day or two before making it permanent.\n20. Evaluate by checklist: walking paths clear, outlets accessible, Wi\u2011Fi tested, lighting suitable for reading/relaxing, screen placement supports sleep goals, cables tidy, dresser and storage functional.\n\nWeekend plan suggestion\n- Day 1 (Saturday morning): Declutter, measure, sketch 2\u20133 layout options. Move small items and declutter.\n- Day 1 (afternoon): Move bed and nightstands into chosen spot. Set up reading lamp and Echo Show; test voice and lighting.\n- Day 2 (Sunday morning): Move dresser/TV/desk; implement cable management as you connect devices. Test Wi\u2011Fi and device placement.\n- Day 2 (afternoon): Finalize decor, mirrors, hooks, and sto