t surfaces with a damp microfiber cloth (to capture dust rather than spread it).\n   - Wash removable covers, pet bedding, and any throws in hot water.\n5. Improve air handling:\n   - Replace HVAC/room air filters (use high-MERV/HEPA-rated filters if compatible).\n   - Run a HEPA air purifier in the living room, especially when you\u2019re there.\n   - If humidity is high, use a dehumidifier (dust mites and mold thrive in humidity).\n6. Reduce pet dander exposure:\n   - Groom your cat regularly (outside or in a well-ventilated area).\n   - Keep the cat off sofas/bedroom if possible, or use washable covers.\n7. Check for mold and irritants:\n   - Inspect vents, behind furniture, and window sills for mold or water damage. If you find mold, address moisture source and clean safely or hire pros.\n   - Avoid strong chemical cleaners and air fresheners that can trigger sneezing; use mild, fragrance-free products.\n8. Monitor improvement: after cleaning and changes, track your sneezing for a week. If symptoms improve, you\u2019ve likely identified the living room as the source.\n9. If symptoms persist or are severe: consider trying an OTC antihistamine (if appropriate for you) and make an appointment with your primary care provider or an allergist. An allergist can offer skin or blood testing and targeted advice (and treatments like immunotherapy if needed).\n10. Long-term: maintain a cleaning routine (regular vacuuming, washing fabrics, filter changes), keep clutter low, and run an air purifier during high-allergen seasons.\n\nIf you want, tell me:\n- When your sneezing tends to occur (only when you\u2019re in the living room?), and\n- Whether you\u2019ve tried any of the cleaning steps yet,\nand I\u2019ll help you prioritize the most effective next action.", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "label": true}}
